  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "A number \\(x\\) is \\(2\\) more than the product of its reciprocal and its additive inverse. In which interval does the number lie?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(-4 \\le x \\le -2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(-2 \\lt x \\le 0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(0 \\lt x \\le 2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(2 \\lt x \\le 4\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(4 \\lt x \\le 6\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10-2001-p2.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The reciprocal of \\(x\\) is \\(\\dfrac1x\\) and its additive inverse is \\(-x.\\) Their product is \\(\\dfrac1x\\cdot(-x)=-1.\\)\n\nSo \\(x=2+(-1)=1,\\) which lies in the interval \\(0\\lt x\\le2.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
